read_theory — flagged tool mirror (NOT scheduled)
==================================================

status: flagged (2026-07-12), not planned for implementation.
spec: "resource-read tool mirrors" under "mcp resources".

what it is: a builtin tool alias for the isabelle://theory/{name}
resource, motivated by uneven mcp-client support for resources —
tools are model-callable everywhere, resources sometimes only via
client UI. NOT built now: the known clients read resources, and a
mirror is a second name for the model to choose between. trigger to
revisit: an evals/ failure where an agent cannot reach theory source
through resources/read.

advertised metadata (sketch)
----------------------------

name          read_theory
description   "Read a theory's source text. Accepts any theory the
              session structure knows — image, loaded, or filesystem
              tier (AFP included when registered). `lines` selects a
              window, e.g. \"120-180\"; without it an oversized
              theory returns the first window plus a truncation
              note. Same content as the isabelle://theory/{name}
              resource."
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {
                 "name": {"type": "string"},
                 "lines": {"type": "string"}},
               "required": ["name"]}
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": true, "idempotentHint": true,
               "openWorldHint": false}

backing: zero new mechanism. the handler is a thin call into the
same resolution path resources/read uses for isabelle://theory/{name}
(image_theory normalization -> tier resolution -> Ir.source /
snapshot node source / session-structure path map; ?lines= slicing
and truncation rules included). if implemented, factor the uri
handler so tool and resource share one function — the tool must
never grow behavior the resource lacks, or the two surfaces drift.

tests (sketch): [scala unit] row + dispatch + parity with the
resource read on Fake_Backend; [e2e] read a filesystem-tier theory
by tool and by resource, assert byte-identical payloads.
